 ## Background
-The integration of geometric priors into NeRF-based reconstruction emerged to address the inherent ambiguity of implicit volumetric representations, particularly in sparse-view settings or scenes with low texture. Early NeRF implementations  relied on photometric consistency alone, which often resulted in "floaters" or incorrect geometry in unobserved regions. Subsequent works introduced explicit geometric signals such as SfM features , monocular depth estimators , and SDFs  to regularize the optimization landscape. This family represents the convergence of traditional multi-view stereo (MVS) techniques with differentiable rendering, allowing for metric-scale reconstruction and direct mesh extraction.
+The integration of geometric priors into NeRF-based reconstruction emerged to address the inherent ambiguity of implicit volumetric representations, particularly in sparse-view settings or scenes with low texture [G]. Early NeRF implementations relied on photometric consistency alone, which often resulted in "floaters" or incorrect geometry in unobserved regions [G]. Subsequent works introduced explicit geometric signals such as SfM features, monocular depth estimators, and SDFs to regularize the optimization landscape [G]. This family represents the convergence of traditional multi-view stereo (MVS) techniques with differentiable rendering, allowing for metric-scale reconstruction and direct mesh extraction [G].
 
